WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.310
- Welcome to the second of two

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:11.980
Franke Visiting Fellow lectures,

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.970
our opportunity to share with
the larger Yale community

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:19.423
work in progress by Alejandra Oliva.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.300
Before I introduce Alejandra,

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:25.550
I'd like to say a few
words about Richard Franke

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:29.120
whose generosity has brought
Alejandra to Yale this year

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.600
and who died last Friday.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:34.390
Richard's contributions to
the Whitney Humanities Center

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:38.570
and its programs have and will
continue to lift our spirits

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:40.510
and nourish our minds.

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:44.320
He provided both the
inspiration and the endowments

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.940
for the Franke Lectures and
Seminars in the Humanities,

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:50.420
the Franke Program in
Science and Humanities,

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:53.570
and the Franke Visiting Fellows Program.

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:57.570
Since 2005, the Franke Visiting Fellows

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:01.060
have served as catalysts
for intellectual gatherings

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:04.710
at the Whitney Humanities
Center setting their own agendas

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:07.490
and presenting their
work to the community.

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:11.550
Their presence ensures ongoing
interdisciplinary exchange

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:14.560
and creative debate at
the Whitney in particular,

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.950
and at Yale in general.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.207
Franke Visiting Fellows
are an integral part

00:01:19.207 --> 00:01:21.900
of the Whitney Fellowship.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:24.240
And this year I've been
especially grateful

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.080
to James McAuley and Alejandra Oliva

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:28.780
for their supportive presence

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:33.600
in our Wednesday Whitney's
Humanities Center Fellows Forums.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.799
One final word about Rich Franke,

00:01:35.799 --> 00:01:39.830
his New York Times obituary
in yesterday's paper

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:44.040
suggests donations to a
cause that was dear to him,

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.589
support for immigrants and refugees,

00:01:46.589 --> 00:01:51.589
specifically Integrated Refugee
and Immigration Services

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:54.550
in New Haven, IRIS.

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:56.820
Thinking of Rich Franke today,

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:59.587
it brings me great pleasure
to be introducing someone

00:01:59.587 --> 00:02:03.310
whose life work centers on immigration

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:05.660
and who does that work in Chicago,

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:08.440
the city where the Frankes made their home

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:10.310
for most of their lives.

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:13.926
I'm delighted then to
introduce Alejandra Oliva.

00:02:13.926 --> 00:02:16.040
Since she's come to the Whitney,

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:18.350
Alejandra has completed the manuscript

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:21.840
of her much anticipated
book "River Mouth,"

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.214
which her publisher has
described beautifully as, quote,

00:02:25.214 --> 00:02:28.940
an urgent reckoning and reexamination

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:30.810
of the humanitarian crisis

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:34.520
we call the American immigration system

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:38.313
based on her work as a
translator for asylum seekers.

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:42.360
Her experiences with borders and asylum

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:47.220
include her time following
a migrant caravan

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.058
and helping families cross over

00:02:49.058 --> 00:02:51.960
from Tijuana into California,

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:55.000
which she described in
an award-winning essay,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.180
at the border no one can know your name.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:01.350
She can also testify to
her work as an interviewer

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.630
at an immigrant detention center

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:07.313
attached to a private prison
in the State of Mississippi.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:11.904
Alejandra brings to all
her work her unique talents

00:03:11.904 --> 00:03:16.904
as a poet, an essayist, a
translator, a textile artist,

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:21.360
a theorist of social justice
and language justice.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.690
Her trajectory is as
intentional and creative

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:27.430
as her artwork.

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:29.740
After an undergraduate degree at Columbia,

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:32.690
she began working with a
new sanctuary coalition

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:37.630
as a volunteer interpreter and
translator in asylum clinics.

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:40.838
She took a master's degree at
the Harvard Divinity School

00:03:40.838 --> 00:03:43.716
where she honed her thinking
about social justice

00:03:43.716 --> 00:03:48.716
reading Oscar Romero and
Valeria Luiselli among others.

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:53.250
The day we discussed
the poster for her talk,

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:56.750
I learned something precious
from her about writing.

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:59.270
She chose to illustrate this lecture,

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:01.550
and you can see the poster over there,

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:04.310
with a work of embroidery she created

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:07.640
at the very worst moment of the lockdown.

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:12.030
Growing beans in her apartment,
sitting in for a long haul,

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:15.730
she decided to sew
individual beans in a pattern

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:17.650
onto the center of a cloth.

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:20.650
And if you look very
closely at the photograph,

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:22.130
you can see the threads

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:24.730
pushed through the beans with a needle,

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:26.730
an arduous process.

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:29.640
She surrounded the
beans with stitched fans

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:30.870
inspired by the work

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.776
of one of her favorite
Mexican textile artists,

00:04:33.776 --> 00:04:36.780
Gabriela Martinez Ortiz.

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:40.103
Two textures, two substances in harmony.

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:43.500
I learned that there's
a link for Alejandra

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:46.441
between putting words
on a page and stitching,

00:04:46.441 --> 00:04:51.163
both involve minute
attention, repeated gestures,

00:04:51.163 --> 00:04:54.930
both are born of a mind
clearing away distractions

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:57.083
and paying attention to detail.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.248
Strength of imagination goes hand in hand

00:05:00.248 --> 00:05:02.130
for Alejandra Oliva

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:05.100
with strength of purpose
in the public sphere.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:09.634
She has worked since 2019 as
the communications coordinator

00:05:09.634 --> 00:05:13.940
for the National Immigrant
Justice Center in Chicago,

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:17.859
which provides direct services,
state and national advocacy,

00:05:17.859 --> 00:05:20.150
and information to media

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:22.740
who are covering exceptionally troubled

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:25.204
and troubling immigration policy.

00:05:25.204 --> 00:05:28.310
We're honored at the Whitney
to have given her space

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:30.860
to write about this important work.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:32.270
Her lecture is entitled

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:34.940
You Can't Translate What You Can't See:

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:38.130
Between Languages in the
U.S. Immigration System.

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:40.742
Please join me in welcoming Alejandra.

00:05:40.742 --> 00:05:43.909
(audience applauding)

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:54.060
- Thank you so, so much Alice

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:56.220
for that incredibly lovely introduction

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:59.330
and thank you so much to the
Whitney Humanities Center

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:00.870
and Mr. and Mrs. Richard Franke

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:03.080
for giving me this incredible gift of time

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:05.350
and resources to work on my book.

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:07.830
I was so sorry to hear of
the passing of Mr. Franke,

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:10.950
may his memory and his
legacy be a blessing.

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:12.920
Thank you also to all of
you that are here today,

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:14.570
particularly my family and my friends

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:16.510
who have traveled to be here.

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:18.978
I'm incredibly excited to
be with you this afternoon

00:06:18.978 --> 00:06:20.350
and to talk to you a little bit

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:23.673
about what I've been working
on during my time here.

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:26.246
As Alice mentioned, I'm a translator.

00:06:26.246 --> 00:06:28.420
I have translated for people

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:30.300
who are filling out asylum applications

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:31.850
in the basement of a church

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:33.920
at the US-Mexico border in Tijuana

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:36.020
for people who are waiting to cross,

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:38.270
for the lucky few who
emerged on the other side

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:40.940
of harrowing legal journeys
who want to tell a journalist

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:42.840
about what happened to them.

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:45.290
I've observed translation
happening over language line,

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:46.576
a telephonic translation service,

00:06:46.576 --> 00:06:49.160
in immigration courtrooms in Boston

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:52.160
for people appearing in
court over video link.

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:54.380
I've witnessed the effects
of a lack of language support

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:57.350
for immigrants incarcerated at
a prison owned by core civic

00:06:57.350 --> 00:06:59.221
in a small town in Mississippi.

00:06:59.221 --> 00:07:00.850
I've also done translation

00:07:00.850 --> 00:07:02.860
in the ways that many of
you have experienced it,

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:04.790
behind a computer screen with a beloved,

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:07.940
or a complicated or exciting
text open in one window,

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:10.090
a word document in another.

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:11.170
Today, I want to talk about

00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:12.700
both of these kinds of translation

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:14.390
and the way that they
sit next to each other

00:07:14.390 --> 00:07:15.653
and influence each other.

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:17.920
I think it's also important to note

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:21.370
that I haven't really gotten
any training as a translator

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:24.270
or an interpreter nor
am I really an academic.

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:26.190
I'd like to think that I've
done a fair amount of reading

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:27.790
in the field of translation studies

00:07:27.790 --> 00:07:29.190
and that my near daily practice

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:30.660
of translation and interpretation

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:33.120
have really helped me sort
through what I've read

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:35.103
to find and embrace what feels true.

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:38.120
But I also know that
I'm not really engaged

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:39.810
with the ways in which
the academic community

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:42.120
has been thinking and
talking about translation.

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:43.148
Because of that I'm not sure how

00:07:43.148 --> 00:07:45.100
or whether my project really speaks

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:47.349
to more contemporary
conversations about translation

00:07:47.349 --> 00:07:49.070
and translation theory,

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:50.880
but I'm really looking
forward to your feedback

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:52.730
and questions at the end of the talk.

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:56.240
When you're translating,
you're constrained

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:58.090
by what you can see on the page.

00:07:58.090 --> 00:07:58.930
If you miss the way

00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:00.935
the two words flow
sibilantly into one another

00:08:00.935 --> 00:08:03.510
or you don't know the
meaning of an aphorism,

00:08:03.510 --> 00:08:04.343
then they don't turn up

00:08:04.343 --> 00:08:06.620
in the version of the text
you create as you translate,

00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:09.350
or if they do, it's a lucky accident.

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:11.470
A sentence carefully crafted
for the way it sounds

00:08:11.470 --> 00:08:12.660
read aloud falls flat.

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:15.780
The sky literally begins
to rain, cats, and dogs.

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:17.819
This kind of mistake is
invisible to most readers

00:08:17.819 --> 00:08:20.800
who will never read both the
original and the translation

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:23.470
who probably don't know
enough of the source language

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:26.140
to clock a slobby translated Axiom.

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:28.010
However, this is also the kind of mistake

00:08:28.010 --> 00:08:30.250
that might keep you up
at night as a translator.

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:32.310
What injustice did you do to the original?

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:34.690
What nuances in the
text does your audience

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:37.204
have no hope of grasping
because you missed it?

00:08:37.204 --> 00:08:40.060
Something similar happens
with the kind of writing I do.

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:42.780
My work sits somewhere
between journalism and memoir.

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:44.770
It's largely based on my own experiences,

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:47.250
but it's bolstered by research,
by going out into the field,

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:50.440
by meeting people, by talking to people,

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:51.480
this is to some extent

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:53.492
translating the real world into language.

00:08:53.492 --> 00:08:55.880
I can't really write
something I haven't seen

00:08:55.880 --> 00:08:56.713
or lived through.

00:08:56.713 --> 00:08:58.887
I've tried, I'm not that good at it,

00:08:58.887 --> 00:09:02.150
but I also can't transmit
the entire of reality to you

00:09:02.150 --> 00:09:05.030
on the page because I can't see it all.

00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:08.000
Things by necessity will
slip through the cracks.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:10.540
The possibility for this kind
of misreading or blindness

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:12.100
also keeps me up at night.

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:13.893
Am I doing the stories I tell justice?

00:09:13.893 --> 00:09:16.987
Am I is the context that
you then will also miss?

00:09:16.987 --> 00:09:18.339
The stories we tell ourselves,

00:09:18.339 --> 00:09:21.859
the stories we receive
culturally also work as blinders,

00:09:21.859 --> 00:09:25.050
giving us quick mental
shortcuts to a misconception

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:26.710
or letting us take the
way out of the story

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:29.040
that makes us feel the least bad.

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:30.632
We prefer to gloss over complexity

00:09:30.632 --> 00:09:32.490
in preference of a simple story,

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:35.223
easy to tell and
understand and respond to.

00:09:36.190 --> 00:09:37.540
These stories are the equivalent

00:09:37.540 --> 00:09:38.930
of a word for word translation.

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:40.910
They help you get a general
shape down on paper,

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:43.060
but otherwise they're oddly constructed

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:45.010
and ungainly they're easily misunderstood

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:47.743
and they fall apart under
any kind of closer scrutiny.

00:09:48.810 --> 00:09:50.860
So much of our current immigration system,

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:51.940
the stories in the media,

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:54.760
the laws that are written are meant to be

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:56.550
like these word for word translations.

00:09:56.550 --> 00:09:59.000
They supply us with the easy
stories, the bad humbres,

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:00.600
the children in cages and through them

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:03.280
are erased legions of other stories.

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:05.630
Thousands of other contexts
and individual lives

00:10:05.630 --> 00:10:07.830
are papered over or
omitted from the record

00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:09.950
in favor of the easy story.

00:10:09.950 --> 00:10:12.850
It is not hard to be moved
to emotion and action

00:10:12.850 --> 00:10:16.430
by these stories to anger,
to grief or fear to protest.

00:10:16.430 --> 00:10:18.660
But it's just as important
to keep paying attention

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:20.133
even as the big stories run out,

00:10:20.133 --> 00:10:23.630
even as the narratives
unspool into a gray area.

00:10:23.630 --> 00:10:26.270
The very existence of the
immigration laws we have today

00:10:26.270 --> 00:10:27.543
is proof of this.

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:31.467
Our asylum laws specifically
were created to respond

00:10:31.467 --> 00:10:34.770
to a single story and for
narratives outside of that,

00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:38.800
the aid we can offer is hams
by the laws were governed by.

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:42.490
Our concept of asylum on an
international level is based on

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:45.260
ideas about religious and
ethnic persecution that began

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:47.650
during the Holocaust
and are narrowly crafted

00:10:47.650 --> 00:10:50.193
to prevent another genocide and just that.

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:54.270
During the Second World War
national immigration quotas

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:56.660
based themselves on a
story about other nations

00:10:56.660 --> 00:10:59.760
overrunning the United States
with floods of immigrants

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:00.649
kept the Ms St. Louis

00:11:00.649 --> 00:11:02.741
floating off the coast
of the United States

00:11:02.741 --> 00:11:04.820
full of German Jewish refugees

00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:08.120
who couldn't claim a formal
kind of immigration assistance.

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:10.450
Even as the ship floated
close enough to the shore

00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:14.050
for the passengers to hear the
music from Miami nightclubs,

00:11:14.050 --> 00:11:16.130
even as Americans argued
that those on board

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:17.950
should be admitted to the US,

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:20.900
our laws made no space for the
passengers of the St. Louis

00:11:20.900 --> 00:11:23.550
to be granted entry
into the United States.

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:25.130
The St. Louis eventually turned around

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:26.550
and docked again in Europe

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:29.310
resulting in the murder of
a quarter of its passengers

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:31.290
in Nazi death camps.

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:32.950
In response to that great horror,

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:35.350
we have expanded our story
about immigration once

00:11:35.350 --> 00:11:37.238
and we need to expand it again today.

00:11:37.238 --> 00:11:39.650
To gain asylum, you have to convince

00:11:39.650 --> 00:11:42.010
the United States government
that you have a credible fear

00:11:42.010 --> 00:11:43.471
of returning to your home country

00:11:43.471 --> 00:11:46.750
because you are or have been
persecuted by your government

00:11:46.750 --> 00:11:49.761
or an entity, it is
unwilling or unable to stop

00:11:49.761 --> 00:11:52.150
and that this persecution happened

00:11:52.150 --> 00:11:55.620
because you are a member of
a particular social group,

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:57.700
a characteristic of
yourself that you are unable

00:11:57.700 --> 00:12:00.050
or should not be forced to
change your political opinion,

00:12:00.050 --> 00:12:01.600
your religion, your sexuality,

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:03.853
your ethnicity, your race, your gender.

00:12:04.940 --> 00:12:07.310
Our immigration laws and the
story that has inspired them

00:12:07.310 --> 00:12:09.960
since the '50s, keep us from
helping the man from Haiti

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:12.020
who is legitimately
afraid of returning home

00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:13.400
because of rising sea levels

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:15.710
and a lack of local infrastructure.

00:12:15.710 --> 00:12:18.020
The United Nations has
predicted that by 2050,

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:21.850
between 25 million and a
billion people worldwide

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:24.420
will be climate refugees
displaced by droughts,

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:26.770
floods, fires, and rising seas.

00:12:26.770 --> 00:12:28.730
Our immigration laws
forces to treat the man

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:30.840
with a possession charge who
has been in the United States

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:32.810
his whole life differently

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:34.190
than the a man with a possession charge

00:12:34.190 --> 00:12:35.769
who arrived when he was a year old,

00:12:35.769 --> 00:12:39.330
both get a few months, a
couple years in prison.

00:12:39.330 --> 00:12:40.310
But at the end of that time,

00:12:40.310 --> 00:12:42.770
we tell one of them that he
has paid his debt to society

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:44.390
and is able to go home

00:12:44.390 --> 00:12:46.490
while the other is
incarcerated for even longer,

00:12:46.490 --> 00:12:48.680
separated from his family and community

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:52.250
and ultimately sent to a
strange country he barely knows.

00:12:52.250 --> 00:12:54.280
Today's immigration laws
asked us to pretend that

00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:55.760
border cities are safe places

00:12:55.760 --> 00:12:57.780
for people waiting to enter the country

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:00.170
even as the state department
issues travel advisories

00:13:00.170 --> 00:13:02.300
warning US citizens to stay away,

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:04.160
ask us to ignore violence against people

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:07.280
unless it happens under
deeply specific circumstances,

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:10.320
ask us to formulaically and
easily refuse help to those

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:12.616
who were me to actually
listen to their stories

00:13:12.616 --> 00:13:15.137
we would extend a hand to.

00:13:15.137 --> 00:13:18.900
What I'm getting at is this,
you make a personal ethics,

00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:21.380
you make laws the same
way you translate a text,

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:22.530
the same way you write a book,

00:13:22.530 --> 00:13:24.330
you look at the world around you

00:13:24.330 --> 00:13:25.660
and there are things you notice

00:13:25.660 --> 00:13:27.290
and there are things you don't.

00:13:27.290 --> 00:13:28.750
There's the short version of the story

00:13:28.750 --> 00:13:33.580
that you're able to receive and
1,000 longer ones you don't.

00:13:33.580 --> 00:13:35.270
There's the short, sorry.

00:13:35.270 --> 00:13:37.250
If you're lucky you read
the words of other people

00:13:37.250 --> 00:13:38.945
who have looked out on
the world before you,

00:13:38.945 --> 00:13:40.820
who have caught other
things that you didn't,

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:43.100
and you change your mind,
you change your actions,

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:45.351
you start seeing things you
had been blind to before.

00:13:45.351 --> 00:13:47.930
There are many things that
because of the family,

00:13:47.930 --> 00:13:49.890
the place in society that I was born into,

00:13:49.890 --> 00:13:51.970
I had the luxury of learning slowly,

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:55.190
of learning only intellectually
and never emotionally

00:13:55.190 --> 00:13:56.991
until someone who had
learned them the hard way

00:13:56.991 --> 00:13:59.230
was kind enough to show me.

00:13:59.230 --> 00:14:01.641
There are other things,
other experiences that I know

00:14:01.641 --> 00:14:05.980
people to be blind to, that
I felt down to my bones.

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:07.610
The thing I've been
writing and thinking about

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:09.710
for the last five years
and more concretely

00:14:09.710 --> 00:14:12.420
in the last few months here
at Yale is yes, immigration,

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:13.585
yes, translation.

00:14:13.585 --> 00:14:16.330
But it's also reading and rereading,

00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:17.970
going over passages in your life,

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:19.510
pouring over someone else's words

00:14:19.510 --> 00:14:22.880
until you find your own
life irrevocably altered.

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:25.060
Today, I want to talk to you
about the way this happened

00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:25.940
in my own life.

00:14:25.940 --> 00:14:27.260
It starts with translation

00:14:27.260 --> 00:14:30.023
and it starts with the two
languages I was raised in.

00:14:32.130 --> 00:14:35.010
What can I tell you about
growing up in two languages?

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:37.250
What can I tell you about
the that Spanish folded me

00:14:37.250 --> 00:14:39.830
into my family, the house I grew up in?

00:14:39.830 --> 00:14:42.100
What can I tell you about
how English gave me access

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:43.530
to the world outside my house,

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:45.170
allowed me to have a different vocabulary

00:14:45.170 --> 00:14:47.130
to talk about who I am?

00:14:47.130 --> 00:14:49.160
Most of my life is conducted in English,

00:14:49.160 --> 00:14:51.740
that's the language I use to
think, read, write, argue,

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:54.880
calculate tips, chat, swear, name myself.

00:14:54.880 --> 00:14:56.970
But still whenever I hear
Spanish on the street,

00:14:56.970 --> 00:14:59.010
especially a strong Mexican Spanish,

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:01.923
a feeling of tenderness of
family springs up inside me,

00:15:01.923 --> 00:15:05.710
this more than anything
means family to me.

00:15:05.710 --> 00:15:08.240
Even though so much of my life
happens in another language,

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:10.700
this well of feeling that Spanish uncovers

00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:12.550
means that my life lived only in English

00:15:12.550 --> 00:15:14.480
would be incomplete.

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:16.100
Growing up we never really lived anywhere

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:18.910
with big communities of Mexican
families, or if there were,

00:15:18.910 --> 00:15:20.490
we weren't really part of them.

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:22.850
My Spanish was not the
Spanish of backyard barbecues

00:15:22.850 --> 00:15:24.300
or entire weekend spent together

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:25.903
with a herd of other children, my age,

00:15:25.903 --> 00:15:27.810
or the Spanish of telling secrets

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:29.363
under the covers at late night sleepovers,

00:15:29.363 --> 00:15:32.090
it was the Spanish of
deep jokes of my parents

00:15:32.090 --> 00:15:34.600
in 1980 slang, all cleaned up for us.

00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:37.570
The Spanish Guadalupe Pineda and Cri-Cri.

00:15:37.570 --> 00:15:39.530
Translation was a way, the only way

00:15:39.530 --> 00:15:41.590
in which Spanish became social.

00:15:41.590 --> 00:15:44.380
It allowed me to reach beyond
the boundaries of my family.

00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:46.250
I marked the beginning of
my work as a translator

00:15:46.250 --> 00:15:48.240
to January of 2017,

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:50.680
the first week of the
Trump administration.

00:15:50.680 --> 00:15:53.090
Like a lot of other people,
I had found myself at odds

00:15:53.090 --> 00:15:56.100
with everything that week,
strange and restless and anxious.

00:15:56.100 --> 00:15:57.950
And so when a friend
offered me the opportunity

00:15:57.950 --> 00:16:00.640
to translate for asylum seekers
at a weekly Pro Se Clinic,

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:01.983
I said, yes, immediately.

00:16:03.200 --> 00:16:05.480
This is how I found myself
sitting in a church basement,

00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:06.950
just off Washington Square Park

00:16:06.950 --> 00:16:08.480
with a couple of other volunteers

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:09.840
across the table from a woman

00:16:09.840 --> 00:16:11.270
who has only been in the United States

00:16:11.270 --> 00:16:14.090
for about three weeks,
let's call her Myra.

00:16:14.090 --> 00:16:16.160
I'm not actually going to be
telling you the real names

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:18.160
or stories of anyone that I've talked to

00:16:19.510 --> 00:16:21.590
because that's their stories and not mine,

00:16:21.590 --> 00:16:24.290
but also not giving you a
name or a face to the people

00:16:24.290 --> 00:16:26.420
that I've met is one of the
reasons that it's so easy

00:16:26.420 --> 00:16:27.538
to discard the stories we hear.

00:16:27.538 --> 00:16:31.370
So for now, for this talk,
Myra exists, she has a name,

00:16:31.370 --> 00:16:34.290
She's a real person I
spent an afternoon with.

00:16:34.290 --> 00:16:35.510
Her story is not dissimilar

00:16:35.510 --> 00:16:37.040
to those of many people I've worked with

00:16:37.040 --> 00:16:38.800
and she's sitting across the table from me

00:16:38.800 --> 00:16:40.623
a week after Trump took office.

00:16:41.590 --> 00:16:44.230
Between us was an I-589 form known as

00:16:44.230 --> 00:16:46.910
the application for asylum
and withholding of removal.

00:16:46.910 --> 00:16:49.428
And it would be our job
together over the next few hours

00:16:49.428 --> 00:16:52.020
to fill out that form and
petition the government

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:54.530
so that she could stay in United States.

00:16:54.530 --> 00:16:56.650
The question of where Myra
will be allowed to call home

00:16:56.650 --> 00:16:58.420
is so critical because the answer

00:16:58.420 --> 00:17:00.620
is a matter of life or death.

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:02.520
Back in Honduras where she
had lived her whole life

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:05.200
up until a few weeks
ago, her brother was shot

00:17:05.200 --> 00:17:06.613
in front of her and the guy who did it

00:17:06.613 --> 00:17:09.110
told her that she would be next.

00:17:09.110 --> 00:17:10.960
The asylum application is her best shot

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:13.170
at convincing the US
government that her survival

00:17:13.170 --> 00:17:15.870
is dependent on her being
able to stay in the country.

00:17:17.320 --> 00:17:19.050
Even though we're here around this table,

00:17:19.050 --> 00:17:20.750
ready to fill out this
application together,

00:17:20.750 --> 00:17:22.860
all of us, which we did not have to be,

00:17:22.860 --> 00:17:25.090
Myra I'm sure wishes that this translation

00:17:25.090 --> 00:17:27.170
we were working on was unnecessary.

00:17:27.170 --> 00:17:28.720
She would rather, her life had continued

00:17:28.720 --> 00:17:30.540
on its familiar way, her brother alive,

00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:32.850
her family together in on Honduras.

00:17:32.850 --> 00:17:35.190
And I assume the other of
a volunteers here with us

00:17:35.190 --> 00:17:36.890
wish that Myra's presence in this country

00:17:36.890 --> 00:17:39.110
was not to some extent contingent

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:41.550
on the strength of my
translation on the tear jerkiness

00:17:41.550 --> 00:17:43.283
or trauma of Myra's story.

00:17:44.120 --> 00:17:46.710
The US government based on
this form appears to wish

00:17:46.710 --> 00:17:49.820
Myra was not here at all
and yet here we all are,

00:17:49.820 --> 00:17:51.770
Myra, all of us volunteers

00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:54.400
and the US government
collaborators on this translation

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:56.217
that no one really wants.

00:17:56.217 --> 00:17:59.070
My job sitting across the table from Myra

00:17:59.070 --> 00:18:01.550
is to let her talk, to
ask clarifying questions

00:18:01.550 --> 00:18:03.020
and see if through the interview

00:18:03.020 --> 00:18:04.780
new information can be shaken loose,

00:18:04.780 --> 00:18:06.460
placed in a chronic logical order to form

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:10.020
a cohesive narrative
with call as an effect.

00:18:10.020 --> 00:18:12.770
My job is also to take her
words and carry them across

00:18:12.770 --> 00:18:13.920
from the Spanish she has spoken

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:15.560
her entire life into English,

00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:17.757
the language spoken by most
people in the United States

00:18:17.757 --> 00:18:19.890
and critically the language spoken by

00:18:19.890 --> 00:18:21.690
the government officials
at the United States

00:18:21.690 --> 00:18:24.080
citizenship and immigration
services that will read

00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:26.177
and judge her application.

00:18:26.177 --> 00:18:29.100
I'm particularly well suited
to the task of not uniquely,

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:31.900
so I've spoken both English
and Spanish, my entire life,

00:18:31.900 --> 00:18:33.860
my childhood echoing with
parental admonishments

00:18:33.860 --> 00:18:36.770
to repeat what I'm saying in Espanol.

00:18:36.770 --> 00:18:39.710
Spanish was a language for
home for family, for affection

00:18:39.710 --> 00:18:41.750
and love and closeness and
English was the language

00:18:41.750 --> 00:18:45.110
of the outside world of many
of the books I read, of school.

00:18:45.110 --> 00:18:46.930
Growing up in a relatively wealthy family

00:18:46.930 --> 00:18:49.250
as the US-Citizen daughter
of Mexican immigrants,

00:18:49.250 --> 00:18:50.610
I given my languages in the same way

00:18:50.610 --> 00:18:53.110
as I was given my
citizenships as a birthrights,

00:18:53.110 --> 00:18:56.010
motherlands and mother tongues at once.

00:18:56.010 --> 00:18:57.152
In my interactions with people that

00:18:57.152 --> 00:18:59.950
were working towards both
Americanness and English,

00:18:59.950 --> 00:19:02.900
I came to see the outlines
of both as artificial.

00:19:02.900 --> 00:19:04.820
In context where everything was being done

00:19:04.820 --> 00:19:07.610
to divide me politically from
the people I was speaking to,

00:19:07.610 --> 00:19:08.720
it was our shared language

00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:10.980
that allowed us to find shared ground.

00:19:10.980 --> 00:19:12.120
It was in doing the work,

00:19:12.120 --> 00:19:13.653
the translation and the interpretation,

00:19:13.653 --> 00:19:17.116
but most importantly, the
listening that my world shifted,

00:19:17.116 --> 00:19:20.610
the political, moral, emotional
category that encompassed me

00:19:20.610 --> 00:19:23.460
and my family and the people
I loved grew and expanded,

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:27.223
not just in name or in
ideals, but in actual fact.

00:19:28.510 --> 00:19:30.840
Translation for asylum seekers
was the thing that finished

00:19:30.840 --> 00:19:33.460
transforming my tongue
into a political actor,

00:19:33.460 --> 00:19:35.350
finished bridging the
divide between the Spanish,

00:19:35.350 --> 00:19:38.180
inside my head and the Spanish
in the rest of the world.

00:19:38.180 --> 00:19:40.574
Spanish, the language of my
family allowed me to feel my way

00:19:40.574 --> 00:19:42.640
into a kind of intimacy with people

00:19:42.640 --> 00:19:45.011
I knew very little about
allowed me to be useful agent

00:19:45.011 --> 00:19:47.620
in a way I didn't know how to imagine.

00:19:47.620 --> 00:19:50.070
It allowed me to take a thing,
a language of vocabulary,

00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:51.040
a way of speaking,

00:19:51.040 --> 00:19:52.890
that was the province
of my safe childhood.

00:19:52.890 --> 00:19:55.170
And to try to extend it
like a protective mantle

00:19:55.170 --> 00:19:58.120
over the people who wanted the
same for their own children.

00:19:58.961 --> 00:20:01.000
The translation with
Myra was the first time

00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:02.290
I was really able to do that,

00:20:02.290 --> 00:20:04.939
but it also immediately became
clear that our conversation

00:20:04.939 --> 00:20:07.080
would not be protective.

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:10.070
It would not be a matter
of safety or care or love.

00:20:10.070 --> 00:20:12.160
If you're not familiar with the I-589 form

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:14.120
an asylum seeker must fill out,

00:20:14.120 --> 00:20:16.040
the bulk of the form consists in detailing

00:20:16.040 --> 00:20:18.220
whatever traumatic event
has sent someone fleeing

00:20:18.220 --> 00:20:20.400
from their country and
answering why they believe

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:23.770
that event came looking
for them specifically.

00:20:23.770 --> 00:20:26.620
As each of the forms, awful,
stilted did questions land.

00:20:26.620 --> 00:20:28.490
I watch as Myra shrinks
farther and farther

00:20:28.490 --> 00:20:29.650
back in her chair,

00:20:29.650 --> 00:20:32.110
her eyes more often seeking
out the corners of the room,

00:20:32.110 --> 00:20:34.780
her sleeve in danger
of being picked apart.

00:20:34.780 --> 00:20:36.860
I offer tissues even though
she's dry eyed water,

00:20:36.860 --> 00:20:38.690
even though her voice doesn't crack,

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:40.160
a break even though when I ask,

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:42.290
she looks at me with
a grim set to her jaw,

00:20:42.290 --> 00:20:45.290
shakes her head no and so we keep going.

00:20:45.290 --> 00:20:46.290
As I talk to her,

00:20:46.290 --> 00:20:48.210
I'm also talking to the
two other volunteers

00:20:48.210 --> 00:20:50.970
around the table with us
explaining to them in my own voice,

00:20:50.970 --> 00:20:53.730
in my own words, what Myra is telling me

00:20:53.730 --> 00:20:55.790
and they're writing it down.

00:20:55.790 --> 00:20:57.420
So the ways in which I'm hurting Myra,

00:20:57.420 --> 00:21:00.750
by asking her to remember,
do not go to waste.

00:21:00.750 --> 00:21:02.980
I take her words and I translate them,

00:21:02.980 --> 00:21:07.513
I carry them across into the
language of her new country.

00:21:08.500 --> 00:21:10.860
Translation comes from
the Latin translatus,

00:21:10.860 --> 00:21:12.220
to carry across.

00:21:12.220 --> 00:21:14.600
The word itself, implies
an edge, a dividing line.

00:21:14.600 --> 00:21:16.630
To translate means to
carry across languages

00:21:16.630 --> 00:21:18.356
across borders, across cultures.

00:21:18.356 --> 00:21:20.761
Translation is also something
we do with our body,

00:21:20.761 --> 00:21:22.340
something that lives in our blood.

00:21:22.340 --> 00:21:24.490
We move through our mother
tongue into that of a new home,

00:21:24.490 --> 00:21:26.060
we sense the right word or phrase

00:21:26.060 --> 00:21:28.340
with the kind of bodily and
tuition where shades of meaning

00:21:28.340 --> 00:21:30.380
an imprecise logic dwell.

00:21:30.380 --> 00:21:33.240
In his book, "Translator's
Turn" Douglas Robinson

00:21:33.240 --> 00:21:35.110
posits a theory of
translation that is visceral,

00:21:35.110 --> 00:21:37.210
that involves gut
feelings and clammy palms,

00:21:37.210 --> 00:21:40.811
sense residing, not in
cognition, but in sensation.

00:21:40.811 --> 00:21:42.880
I have found this to be true.

00:21:42.880 --> 00:21:44.810
Our bodies remember the specific

00:21:44.810 --> 00:21:46.520
flavor of words in two languages.

00:21:46.520 --> 00:21:48.983
The taste of the edge
between two ways of saying.

00:21:50.290 --> 00:21:52.970
As the word implies we also
use our bodies to carry meaning

00:21:52.970 --> 00:21:55.050
from one language to another.

00:21:55.050 --> 00:21:56.420
It's impossible not to feel

00:21:56.420 --> 00:21:58.220
what I'm translating inside my body,

00:21:58.220 --> 00:21:59.750
impossible not to carry it with me

00:21:59.750 --> 00:22:02.470
from a church basement or a
plaza after I hang up the phone

00:22:02.470 --> 00:22:06.000
damp and hot from being pressed
next to my ear for an hour.

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:08.030
So much of translation and interpretation

00:22:08.030 --> 00:22:10.020
requires me to pass
someone else's language

00:22:10.020 --> 00:22:11.160
through the first person.

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:13.970
Their eyes becomes my own,
their injuries and experiences

00:22:13.970 --> 00:22:16.250
enter language that for all
that I'm trying to render

00:22:16.250 --> 00:22:19.870
faithfully as theirs is
in reality only mine.

00:22:19.870 --> 00:22:21.350
This line of self and not self

00:22:21.350 --> 00:22:22.700
is like chalk on a Blackboard.

00:22:22.700 --> 00:22:24.910
From far away the line looks crisp, stark,

00:22:24.910 --> 00:22:27.170
easy to understand,
but as you approach it,

00:22:27.170 --> 00:22:28.780
as your nose bumps the dust slate,

00:22:28.780 --> 00:22:30.930
you understand that the line
is not exactly unbroken,

00:22:30.930 --> 00:22:32.940
that no matter how much you erase,

00:22:32.940 --> 00:22:34.580
the particles of the
chalk that wear that line

00:22:34.580 --> 00:22:36.450
still float through the air.

00:22:36.450 --> 00:22:38.710
When you translate, it becomes
clear that to be entangled

00:22:38.710 --> 00:22:41.120
with someone's language is
to be entangled in them,

00:22:41.120 --> 00:22:42.930
in their histories and idiosyncrasies.

00:22:42.930 --> 00:22:45.463
To translate is to be in
community with someone.

00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:48.110
This is in part because
language is something

00:22:48.110 --> 00:22:50.140
we construct together across countries

00:22:50.140 --> 00:22:51.480
and continents and centuries,

00:22:51.480 --> 00:22:54.170
as much as within communities
and conversations.

00:22:54.170 --> 00:22:56.419
There is no expression, no
understanding without the other

00:22:56.419 --> 00:22:59.690
to express or to understand.

00:22:59.690 --> 00:23:00.670
When I speak Spanish,

00:23:00.670 --> 00:23:04.140
I'm speaking the language of
Hernan Cortes and La Malinche,

00:23:04.140 --> 00:23:07.080
Sor Juana and Gioconda
Belli, Servantis and Marquez.

00:23:07.080 --> 00:23:08.150
It's also my own language,

00:23:08.150 --> 00:23:09.440
the words taught to me by my mother

00:23:09.440 --> 00:23:11.381
when I was just learning
to ask for what I wanted,

00:23:11.381 --> 00:23:14.140
the made up language composed
of malapropisms of mine

00:23:14.140 --> 00:23:16.418
and my siblings as we
were learning to talk,

00:23:16.418 --> 00:23:19.590
the borrowed words from English,
the patterns of sentences

00:23:19.590 --> 00:23:22.140
I unknowingly absorbed from
my parents' ways of speaking

00:23:22.140 --> 00:23:23.810
and made my own.

00:23:23.810 --> 00:23:25.890
This language is the water I stand in,

00:23:25.890 --> 00:23:27.114
the well I draw from.

00:23:27.114 --> 00:23:29.700
There are tributaries
feeding my spot in the river,

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:31.204
both above and below the earth.

00:23:31.204 --> 00:23:33.650
This same river, a little further upstream

00:23:33.650 --> 00:23:35.610
is the one the 17th century poet nun,

00:23:35.610 --> 00:23:37.620
Sor Juana Ines de la Cruz drew from.

00:23:37.620 --> 00:23:39.970
And when I say the word
muher or the word fe,

00:23:39.970 --> 00:23:41.910
these are words that have
been fundamentally altered

00:23:41.910 --> 00:23:44.980
by her having written about
them some 300 years ago.

00:23:44.980 --> 00:23:47.730
The Spanish she wrote in turn
was influenced by her time

00:23:47.730 --> 00:23:50.020
in the viceroy's court,
as a lady in waiting

00:23:50.020 --> 00:23:51.530
as it was by the Nahuatl she learned

00:23:51.530 --> 00:23:54.180
from her mother's
household staff as a child.

00:23:54.180 --> 00:23:56.200
As influenced by the
Spanish of Hernan Cortes

00:23:56.200 --> 00:23:57.370
who had first come to Mexico

00:23:57.370 --> 00:24:00.010
just 129 years before her birth.

00:24:00.010 --> 00:24:01.610
As by the language of Malinche,

00:24:01.610 --> 00:24:03.550
the Nahua woman who
served as his interpreter

00:24:03.550 --> 00:24:05.610
and the mother of one of his children.

00:24:05.610 --> 00:24:07.240
This was the water Sor Juana drew from

00:24:07.240 --> 00:24:08.530
and what she returned to the river

00:24:08.530 --> 00:24:12.070
was forever altered by having
passed through her hands.

00:24:12.070 --> 00:24:13.160
When I speak or write,

00:24:13.160 --> 00:24:14.920
my words flew out from my own little Eddie

00:24:14.920 --> 00:24:16.603
to rejoin the wider stream.

00:24:18.230 --> 00:24:20.220
My turns of phrase become
part of the Spanish

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:22.380
that exists in the world
just as Sor Juana's did,

00:24:22.380 --> 00:24:24.110
able to be picked up and passed around,

00:24:24.110 --> 00:24:27.820
drawn up to drink and to be
appropriated by another waiter.

00:24:27.820 --> 00:24:29.980
In this way language is
made up of the personal

00:24:29.980 --> 00:24:30.870
and the cumulative.

00:24:30.870 --> 00:24:32.420
Each of us standing in our own space,

00:24:32.420 --> 00:24:33.740
absorbing the words around us

00:24:33.740 --> 00:24:36.120
and contributing to the greater stream.

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:38.930
Like the waters of the river,
it's an inevitable inseparable

00:24:38.930 --> 00:24:41.330
commingling of the collective
and the individual.

00:24:41.330 --> 00:24:43.300
Language would not work
if we could not share it,

00:24:43.300 --> 00:24:44.850
but the individual impressions we leave

00:24:44.850 --> 00:24:46.190
and the baggage we bring with us

00:24:46.190 --> 00:24:48.173
on each word we say matter too.

00:24:49.120 --> 00:24:51.020
We should also talk a
little bit about the baggage

00:24:51.020 --> 00:24:52.870
that the translation
in and of itself brings

00:24:52.870 --> 00:24:54.510
particularly here in the West.

00:24:54.510 --> 00:24:56.790
A lot of the earliest questions
in translation studies

00:24:56.790 --> 00:24:59.940
are ultimately biblical
translation theory.

00:24:59.940 --> 00:25:02.520
This is why even when you're
not necessarily talking about

00:25:02.520 --> 00:25:04.990
the word of God, the original
is held up as this bright

00:25:04.990 --> 00:25:08.580
and viable that the translator
must struggle to approach

00:25:08.580 --> 00:25:10.140
much less replicate.

00:25:10.140 --> 00:25:11.610
To find the source of this brightness,

00:25:11.610 --> 00:25:14.290
theorists keep pointing, not
just behind the original text,

00:25:14.290 --> 00:25:16.062
but behind language itself.

00:25:16.062 --> 00:25:17.863
Walter Benjamin, who is deeply concerned

00:25:17.863 --> 00:25:20.437
with the idea of God's
language in his essay,

00:25:20.437 --> 00:25:23.510
"The Task of the Translator,"
describes a good translation

00:25:23.510 --> 00:25:25.670
as one that is transparent
so that it, quote,

00:25:25.670 --> 00:25:28.550
does not cover up the original,
does not block its light,

00:25:28.550 --> 00:25:29.910
but allows the pure language

00:25:29.910 --> 00:25:32.470
to shine upon the original all the more.

00:25:32.470 --> 00:25:35.460
He gestures not only at
the pure language of able

00:25:35.460 --> 00:25:37.560
and the Bible, but to the threshold space

00:25:37.560 --> 00:25:38.810
that translation occupies

00:25:38.810 --> 00:25:40.913
the way it needs to both exist and not.

00:25:41.800 --> 00:25:43.790
There's a sense in which
looking at any original

00:25:43.790 --> 00:25:46.410
in the light of divine
origination makes total sense.

00:25:46.410 --> 00:25:49.720
The creation of any text is
a miracle of individuation,

00:25:49.720 --> 00:25:51.130
of reference and self-reference

00:25:51.130 --> 00:25:54.191
of idiosyncratic experiences
just as miraculous

00:25:54.191 --> 00:25:57.200
as the idea of an
individual consciousness.

00:25:57.200 --> 00:25:58.770
When you're translating literary text,

00:25:58.770 --> 00:26:00.590
there's a kind of slow awareness of this

00:26:00.590 --> 00:26:02.920
as you find your sentences
unfurling along paths

00:26:02.920 --> 00:26:04.500
that are not your usual,

00:26:04.500 --> 00:26:07.050
a different understanding
of the creator of the text.

00:26:07.050 --> 00:26:10.300
When you become aware that
this author, your author,

00:26:10.300 --> 00:26:11.457
uses the word, the word
querto, the word casa,

00:26:11.457 --> 00:26:15.491
the word plata, like no
one ever has or will again,

00:26:15.491 --> 00:26:18.800
you also realize that you can
only ever know a part of it,

00:26:18.800 --> 00:26:21.170
you can only skim the
surface of their meaning,

00:26:21.170 --> 00:26:23.870
the dizzy individuality of
any single writer or mind

00:26:23.870 --> 00:26:26.227
multiplied by all the
possible writers and minds,

00:26:26.227 --> 00:26:29.590
or even your own as you read
or turn unfolds into a world

00:26:29.590 --> 00:26:32.430
that feels as intricate
as God's fingerprint.

00:26:32.430 --> 00:26:34.801
My investment in this
idea however stops short

00:26:34.801 --> 00:26:36.800
when I think of my other source text,

00:26:36.800 --> 00:26:38.960
which are not usually the
product of slow mornings

00:26:38.960 --> 00:26:41.530
sitting at your desk, or
even the stress and pressure

00:26:41.530 --> 00:26:43.480
of looming deadlines,
but rather the product

00:26:43.480 --> 00:26:45.720
of extreme violence and fear created

00:26:45.720 --> 00:26:47.410
not to serve a creative desire,

00:26:47.410 --> 00:26:50.130
but rather to fill a bureaucrats form.

00:26:50.130 --> 00:26:52.430
I don't like the implication
that there's something holy

00:26:52.430 --> 00:26:53.630
about suffering that's present

00:26:53.630 --> 00:26:55.250
in so much of Christian philosophy

00:26:55.250 --> 00:26:58.320
and often gets carried over
into translation theory.

00:26:58.320 --> 00:27:00.740
I hate the idea that there's
a pure and shining light

00:27:00.740 --> 00:27:03.850
at the back of any of the
pain I've born witness to.

00:27:03.850 --> 00:27:05.880
None of it feels like
what I want God to be.

00:27:05.880 --> 00:27:08.400
I don't want to honor the
original don't want to revere it

00:27:08.400 --> 00:27:10.335
like I might the words from God's mouth.

00:27:10.335 --> 00:27:13.160
On the other hand, I also
don't want to mutilate

00:27:13.160 --> 00:27:15.080
the stories I'm given like I do every time

00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:16.790
I fill out an asylum application,

00:27:16.790 --> 00:27:18.810
it feels sacrilegious
to cut up someone's life

00:27:18.810 --> 00:27:20.400
in the interest of the government

00:27:20.400 --> 00:27:22.422
because there's something
of value in someone's story.

00:27:22.422 --> 00:27:24.770
Something that deserves careful treatment,

00:27:24.770 --> 00:27:26.550
deserves to be honored and respected

00:27:26.550 --> 00:27:29.550
and not prized out with
priors and smashed up.

00:27:29.550 --> 00:27:31.640
This something isn't about the divinity

00:27:31.640 --> 00:27:33.850
or the fore ordination of any story

00:27:33.850 --> 00:27:36.350
or the sacredness of the
idea of stories in general,

00:27:36.350 --> 00:27:38.290
or about the beauty of suffering.

00:27:38.290 --> 00:27:39.720
But because the people telling them

00:27:39.720 --> 00:27:42.760
are viscerally embodied human.

00:27:42.760 --> 00:27:45.069
As I'm reminded with every
tissue or glass of water,

00:27:45.069 --> 00:27:46.740
these stories are important

00:27:46.740 --> 00:27:48.037
because the person sitting in front of me

00:27:48.037 --> 00:27:50.156
and I share the fragility of our flesh

00:27:50.156 --> 00:27:52.080
and are entrusted to one another

00:27:52.080 --> 00:27:53.670
because of the thinness of our skins,

00:27:53.670 --> 00:27:55.883
the delicate workings of our insides.

00:27:56.880 --> 00:27:59.810
At the same time our
bodies are not enough.

00:27:59.810 --> 00:28:02.160
The asylum application is a skeleton that,

00:28:02.160 --> 00:28:05.790
like the worst be movie
cliche longs for a body.

00:28:05.790 --> 00:28:08.920
The asylum system is hungry
for bodies, bodies to suffer,

00:28:08.920 --> 00:28:11.171
bodies to fill up beds in
for profit detention centers,

00:28:11.171 --> 00:28:13.200
bodies to work in the fields and factories

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:15.130
and behind the scenes of everything,

00:28:15.130 --> 00:28:18.050
all the while rejecting their humanity.

00:28:18.050 --> 00:28:20.680
The I-589 pretends to
care about biography,

00:28:20.680 --> 00:28:22.767
asks for a narrative in a
specific person's voice.

00:28:22.767 --> 00:28:25.170
But in reality is only concerned

00:28:25.170 --> 00:28:26.550
with the violence on the body,

00:28:26.550 --> 00:28:29.490
glancing off anything
deeper than the skin.

00:28:29.490 --> 00:28:31.180
A bad translation does the same work,

00:28:31.180 --> 00:28:32.760
it lives on the surface of the text,

00:28:32.760 --> 00:28:35.010
it's concerned with a physical
shape, the surface meaning,

00:28:35.010 --> 00:28:38.140
whatever reflection
momentarily blinds you.

00:28:38.140 --> 00:28:39.470
A good translation drives

00:28:39.470 --> 00:28:41.485
somewhere to the beating heart of a text.

00:28:41.485 --> 00:28:43.750
You can only translate what you can see

00:28:43.750 --> 00:28:46.090
and if all that is visible
to you is a somebody,

00:28:46.090 --> 00:28:47.571
if all that is presented is the surface,

00:28:47.571 --> 00:28:49.420
then there's something about the human

00:28:49.420 --> 00:28:51.560
that's still invisible to
you, to the government,

00:28:51.560 --> 00:28:53.330
to the immigration judge in his chambers

00:28:53.330 --> 00:28:55.413
and the CBP patrolman in his truck.

00:28:56.950 --> 00:28:59.930
The people I translate for are
survivors among generations

00:28:59.930 --> 00:29:01.920
of the sabatassidos, the farmers with land

00:29:01.920 --> 00:29:04.960
not yet seized or made barren
by multinational corporations,

00:29:04.960 --> 00:29:06.850
the people who are in a out
left to die in the desert

00:29:06.850 --> 00:29:09.000
or dumped into mass graves in the sea

00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:11.180
or corralled into camps to die of neglect.

00:29:11.180 --> 00:29:13.273
The compassino no one bothered to bribe.

00:29:14.160 --> 00:29:15.900
The United States has been deeply involved

00:29:15.900 --> 00:29:17.680
in Central and South America for decades.

00:29:17.680 --> 00:29:18.910
Behind every revolution,

00:29:18.910 --> 00:29:20.980
every uprising coup of the 20th century,

00:29:20.980 --> 00:29:22.670
there were CIA agents or Congressmen

00:29:22.670 --> 00:29:25.947
making colonial decisions to
stop the spread of communism

00:29:25.947 --> 00:29:30.530
or more commonly to protect US
investments and corporations.

00:29:30.530 --> 00:29:31.820
The translation I take part in

00:29:31.820 --> 00:29:33.670
is the meagres form of reparations

00:29:33.670 --> 00:29:36.260
and has more to do with the
CIA agents and the Congressman

00:29:36.260 --> 00:29:37.310
than the utopian promise

00:29:37.310 --> 00:29:39.713
that more contemporary
translators make for us.

00:29:40.780 --> 00:29:43.140
Like the United States, which
welcomed European immigrants

00:29:43.140 --> 00:29:44.993
as low level workers with
a plea for the world's

00:29:44.993 --> 00:29:48.330
tired and hungry, which builds
itself as a great melting pot

00:29:48.330 --> 00:29:50.638
with fine words about
the equality of all men,

00:29:50.638 --> 00:29:53.580
translation likes to describe
itself as a diplomat,

00:29:53.580 --> 00:29:55.790
bridging cultures, or as a bridge itself

00:29:55.790 --> 00:29:57.000
spanning the Rio Grande,

00:29:57.000 --> 00:29:59.490
the kind of thing that
can transcend borders,

00:29:59.490 --> 00:30:01.324
but ignoring the ways
power can come through

00:30:01.324 --> 00:30:05.140
and across that bridge, the
way a diplomat always, always

00:30:05.140 --> 00:30:07.320
has his own nation's
best interests at heart,

00:30:07.320 --> 00:30:09.313
you become complicit in this power.

00:30:10.830 --> 00:30:13.010
When I translate for an
asylum seeker like Myra,

00:30:13.010 --> 00:30:15.830
I like to think I'm working
against the powers that be,

00:30:15.830 --> 00:30:17.940
but the truth is I'm filling out the form.

00:30:17.940 --> 00:30:20.690
I'm making people findable,
searchable, cross indexable

00:30:20.690 --> 00:30:22.810
for a government that has
proven time and time again,

00:30:22.810 --> 00:30:25.390
that the only way in which
it cares about these lives

00:30:25.390 --> 00:30:28.849
is as resources to be
extracted to exhaustion.

00:30:28.849 --> 00:30:30.960
I don't know how else to help.

00:30:30.960 --> 00:30:33.310
I don't know if I'm brave
or smart or strong enough

00:30:33.310 --> 00:30:36.080
to find another way to wipe
away the fear of separation,

00:30:36.080 --> 00:30:38.430
the fear of return, to remake
the world into the place

00:30:38.430 --> 00:30:40.760
it needs to be to make
sure everyone is safe.

00:30:40.760 --> 00:30:42.700
I don't even know if bravery
or smarts or strength

00:30:42.700 --> 00:30:44.850
on an individual or an
even collective level

00:30:44.850 --> 00:30:47.036
are capable of fixing this problem.

00:30:47.036 --> 00:30:49.075
Instead, what I have
is this government form

00:30:49.075 --> 00:30:51.727
and a little while to spend
across the table from Myra

00:30:51.727 --> 00:30:53.929
and the ability to make her feel heard,

00:30:53.929 --> 00:30:58.059
even if I know this application
might not be enough.

00:30:58.059 --> 00:31:00.480
Years later when I started
working more closely

00:31:00.480 --> 00:31:01.920
with actual immigration attorneys

00:31:01.920 --> 00:31:03.240
rather than just volunteers,

00:31:03.240 --> 00:31:06.170
I found out that they actually
called the I-589 on its own

00:31:06.170 --> 00:31:09.530
a skeleton application,
just the bare bones.

00:31:09.530 --> 00:31:11.920
When a lawyer actually helps
someone apply for asylum,

00:31:11.920 --> 00:31:12.753
something that happens

00:31:12.753 --> 00:31:15.520
in less than four and 10
people applying from outside

00:31:15.520 --> 00:31:17.520
the detention center and
for even fewer people

00:31:17.520 --> 00:31:22.112
applying from inside, the
skeleton is in fleshed,

00:31:22.112 --> 00:31:25.330
you add affidavits, expert
witness testimonies,

00:31:25.330 --> 00:31:26.900
reports from forensic pathologies

00:31:26.900 --> 00:31:29.170
who are able to examine
clients living bodies,

00:31:29.170 --> 00:31:32.440
fact files of reporting on
what's called country conditions.

00:31:32.440 --> 00:31:34.310
A fully completed asylum application

00:31:34.310 --> 00:31:38.600
usually runs somewhere
between 300 and 500 pages,

00:31:38.600 --> 00:31:41.010
solid bricks of paperwork,
they're stacked up high

00:31:41.010 --> 00:31:43.892
like a defensive wall when
someone goes into court.

00:31:43.892 --> 00:31:48.820
We in the Pro Se Clinic were
turning in the 12 flimsy pages

00:31:48.820 --> 00:31:51.749
of the application alone,
this in itself was a strategy.

00:31:51.749 --> 00:31:53.910
It allowed Myra to have an application in

00:31:53.910 --> 00:31:55.583
prior to the one year filing deadline.

00:31:55.583 --> 00:31:58.330
It gave her extra time to find an attorney

00:31:58.330 --> 00:31:59.750
to find another means of protection,

00:31:59.750 --> 00:32:01.870
to ensure that she's at least got

00:32:01.870 --> 00:32:04.400
that pending asylum
application, that paperwork in

00:32:04.400 --> 00:32:05.570
as a mean for safeguarding

00:32:05.570 --> 00:32:07.280
against deportation for the years

00:32:07.280 --> 00:32:08.630
it will take for that application

00:32:08.630 --> 00:32:09.983
to wind through the system.

00:32:10.930 --> 00:32:12.730
The text that I end up
producing for that form

00:32:12.730 --> 00:32:15.947
is by necessity skeletal, it's
a bone chart in the desert.

00:32:15.947 --> 00:32:18.860
The boxes provided for
answers to the questions

00:32:18.860 --> 00:32:20.844
asked by the I-589 are small.

00:32:20.844 --> 00:32:22.820
And the attorneys who review our files

00:32:22.820 --> 00:32:26.090
don't recommend using the
over flow space provided.

00:32:26.090 --> 00:32:29.091
So we're restricted to
150 words per answer,

00:32:29.091 --> 00:32:31.300
clipped and factual,

00:32:31.300 --> 00:32:33.090
describing the things
that have left a mark

00:32:33.090 --> 00:32:34.070
on a single body,

00:32:34.070 --> 00:32:36.713
but never what it is like
to live inside that body.

00:32:38.270 --> 00:32:41.400
Asylum translation is less
like building a bridge

00:32:41.400 --> 00:32:44.550
and more like the translation
of saintly relics.

00:32:44.550 --> 00:32:45.800
When a saint is canonized,

00:32:45.800 --> 00:32:47.460
they're brought up out
of their resting place,

00:32:47.460 --> 00:32:49.220
usually somewhere where
they lived and grew up

00:32:49.220 --> 00:32:52.010
and did their holy works and
instead brought to a place

00:32:52.010 --> 00:32:53.310
that is the center of power.

00:32:53.310 --> 00:32:54.750
When Constantinople, for example,

00:32:54.750 --> 00:32:56.189
became the center of the Christian world,

00:32:56.189 --> 00:32:58.630
a flock of holy bones was translated there

00:32:58.630 --> 00:32:59.874
from the surrounding areas.

00:32:59.874 --> 00:33:03.470
This legitimated the holiness
of the tibia of St. Leucadia,

00:33:03.470 --> 00:33:06.080
the forest full of
splinters of the holy cross,

00:33:06.080 --> 00:33:08.760
the skull of St. Cordula
but it also gave holiness

00:33:08.760 --> 00:33:11.810
and legitimacy to the
church in Constantinople,

00:33:11.810 --> 00:33:13.210
marking it as holy for being able

00:33:13.210 --> 00:33:14.828
to gather holiness to itself.

00:33:14.828 --> 00:33:17.070
When saint's bones enter church,

00:33:17.070 --> 00:33:18.270
they're gulden and boxed up,

00:33:18.270 --> 00:33:20.880
removed from their text of
a grave of a living person

00:33:20.880 --> 00:33:24.370
somewhere in a community
and instead recontextualized

00:33:24.370 --> 00:33:27.010
and presented alongside the
calcified hedge geographies

00:33:27.010 --> 00:33:29.150
as proof of the church's power.

00:33:29.150 --> 00:33:32.415
You don't know actually who
St. Leucadia or St. Cordula are

00:33:32.415 --> 00:33:35.340
or who they were or what
they did or how they died.

00:33:35.340 --> 00:33:37.013
All you know are their
bones, their holiness,

00:33:37.013 --> 00:33:40.930
their presence proof that the
church is worth dying for.

00:33:40.930 --> 00:33:42.780
The translation of bones serves the church

00:33:42.780 --> 00:33:44.680
and not the stories of those interred.

00:33:45.640 --> 00:33:48.180
The US government is
not the Catholic church

00:33:48.180 --> 00:33:51.050
but it does require asylum
seekers to be saints.

00:33:51.050 --> 00:33:52.670
It gathers their stories to itself,

00:33:52.670 --> 00:33:55.580
forces them to be translated
in language and in fact,

00:33:55.580 --> 00:33:56.550
given that they don't accept

00:33:56.550 --> 00:33:57.980
anything other than paper files,

00:33:57.980 --> 00:33:59.660
something which has continued to be true

00:33:59.660 --> 00:34:03.330
through the pandemic, mailed
into their headquarters,

00:34:03.330 --> 00:34:06.470
into their own language
and space literally.

00:34:06.470 --> 00:34:08.630
The power here only moves in one direction

00:34:08.630 --> 00:34:10.390
and everything done by asylum seekers,

00:34:10.390 --> 00:34:12.507
every translation done
by them and their allies

00:34:12.507 --> 00:34:15.743
serves only the reader, the
United States government.

00:34:17.480 --> 00:34:19.980
The text, the presentation
of it, the imprisonment of it

00:34:19.980 --> 00:34:21.160
within the forms boxes

00:34:21.160 --> 00:34:22.847
away from the messiness of a real life

00:34:22.847 --> 00:34:24.490
and into the beats of a known

00:34:24.490 --> 00:34:27.720
and understood hedge geography,
a quiet and virtuous life

00:34:27.720 --> 00:34:29.640
shattered by a single act of explicitly,

00:34:29.640 --> 00:34:32.940
politically motivated
violence and escape by night,

00:34:32.940 --> 00:34:35.500
a plea for safety, these
are meant not at all

00:34:35.500 --> 00:34:37.540
as an expression of the
person telling the story

00:34:37.540 --> 00:34:40.860
and entirely to satisfy
the entity processing it.

00:34:40.860 --> 00:34:43.820
If I do my job right,
if in those 150 words

00:34:43.820 --> 00:34:45.930
I succeed as a translator,

00:34:45.930 --> 00:34:47.810
what the asylum seeker
I'm working with gets

00:34:47.810 --> 00:34:50.270
is a grudging acceptance
of their humanity,

00:34:50.270 --> 00:34:52.100
a big official rubber stamp that says

00:34:52.100 --> 00:34:54.960
that their suffering matters,
but not much else does

00:34:54.960 --> 00:34:56.720
about the ways in which
they have stayed alive

00:34:56.720 --> 00:34:59.565
or lived at all, just the suffering.

00:34:59.565 --> 00:35:01.350
A lawyer I worked with at the clinic

00:35:01.350 --> 00:35:03.150
called it bureaucratic violence,

00:35:03.150 --> 00:35:05.810
the carrying across of people
out of their own words,

00:35:05.810 --> 00:35:09.630
their own language, the mutilation
of the stories they tell,

00:35:09.630 --> 00:35:11.330
a violence I participated in,

00:35:11.330 --> 00:35:13.683
even if it was as gentle
as I could make it.

00:35:14.920 --> 00:35:17.710
There was however, one other
way in which the translation

00:35:17.710 --> 00:35:19.600
I was doing was skeletal.

00:35:19.600 --> 00:35:21.080
When you're doing literary translation,

00:35:21.080 --> 00:35:22.570
again, it's common to talk about the ways

00:35:22.570 --> 00:35:23.960
that you're subsumed enveloped,

00:35:23.960 --> 00:35:26.260
given over to the shape of
someone else's language.

00:35:26.260 --> 00:35:28.831
A literary translation project
involves making yourself

00:35:28.831 --> 00:35:31.490
porous to the text, allowing it into you

00:35:31.490 --> 00:35:33.480
and allowing yourself into it.

00:35:33.480 --> 00:35:35.500
All reading, all
interactions really involve

00:35:35.500 --> 00:35:36.966
this kind of cross pollination,

00:35:36.966 --> 00:35:40.530
but translation also asks and
answers a different question.

00:35:40.530 --> 00:35:42.220
When you're translating,
you're giving over room

00:35:42.220 --> 00:35:44.420
in your brain, days of
your one single life

00:35:44.420 --> 00:35:46.641
to the careful minute study
of someone else's work

00:35:46.641 --> 00:35:47.930
in order that others

00:35:47.930 --> 00:35:50.460
might have the privilege
of encountering it.

00:35:50.460 --> 00:35:52.590
You're not only
recognizing that your voice

00:35:52.590 --> 00:35:54.510
isn't the most important one,

00:35:54.510 --> 00:35:57.500
but you're sinking precious
hours into that belief.

00:35:57.500 --> 00:35:59.880
There's also a return though,
in making yourself porous,

00:35:59.880 --> 00:36:01.310
you also make yourself expansive,

00:36:01.310 --> 00:36:03.780
you're allowed to reach beyond yourself.

00:36:03.780 --> 00:36:04.710
This is both different

00:36:04.710 --> 00:36:06.990
and not when I'm translating
for asylum seekers,

00:36:06.990 --> 00:36:08.630
even as I'm translating their stories,

00:36:08.630 --> 00:36:11.690
I'm passing every word they say
through my own first person,

00:36:11.690 --> 00:36:15.240
feeling a echo of what they're
telling me in my own self.

00:36:15.240 --> 00:36:16.800
Their stories are often ones of violence

00:36:16.800 --> 00:36:18.940
against their bodies,
stories that pass through me

00:36:18.940 --> 00:36:20.710
as they pass through language.

00:36:20.710 --> 00:36:22.220
I can catalog on my own body

00:36:22.220 --> 00:36:24.250
the places where people
have shown me their scars,

00:36:24.250 --> 00:36:25.720
turning me into a mirror,

00:36:25.720 --> 00:36:28.347
cigarette burns on the back
of a carefully manicured hand.

00:36:28.347 --> 00:36:30.840
The aftermath of a surgery that was itself

00:36:30.840 --> 00:36:32.140
the aftermath of a stabbing

00:36:32.140 --> 00:36:34.180
someone's chest and the
softness of their belly,

00:36:34.180 --> 00:36:35.690
crisscrossed with ropes of scar

00:36:35.690 --> 00:36:37.840
and the punctum of
stitches on either side,

00:36:39.330 --> 00:36:42.980
old, old bullet scars
cleanly through both ankles.

00:36:42.980 --> 00:36:45.350
An unset broken leg, and
the weight on my shoulder

00:36:45.350 --> 00:36:48.337
of the man who used me as
a crutch for a short walk.

00:36:48.337 --> 00:36:50.720
I don't know in my body
what it's like to be hurt

00:36:50.720 --> 00:36:53.610
in these ways, but I carry
a record with me anyway,

00:36:53.610 --> 00:36:55.453
because it's impossible not to.

00:36:56.560 --> 00:36:58.680
It's very much unvogue right now

00:36:58.680 --> 00:37:00.620
to talk about secondhand trauma,

00:37:00.620 --> 00:37:03.340
something common among
social workers and EMTs

00:37:03.340 --> 00:37:04.810
and anyone else whose job involves

00:37:04.810 --> 00:37:06.737
stepping into the worst
day in people's lives

00:37:06.737 --> 00:37:09.230
as a shadow of knowledge
that wraps around you

00:37:09.230 --> 00:37:11.420
when you have come to
know through observation,

00:37:11.420 --> 00:37:14.430
the depths of cruelty
that exist in the world.

00:37:14.430 --> 00:37:16.010
Secondhand trauma is complex.

00:37:16.010 --> 00:37:17.250
It works in many of the same ways

00:37:17.250 --> 00:37:18.370
that firsthand trauma does,

00:37:18.370 --> 00:37:20.180
your brain gets rewired in the same way

00:37:20.180 --> 00:37:23.370
as your physiological
responses are the same to it.

00:37:23.370 --> 00:37:26.400
However, secondhand trauma also
carries the shame of safety.

00:37:26.400 --> 00:37:27.450
You're aware that you live

00:37:27.450 --> 00:37:29.010
removed from the suffering itself,

00:37:29.010 --> 00:37:31.040
that your reactions are
outsized for someone

00:37:31.040 --> 00:37:33.450
who basically is just
heard about the pain,

00:37:33.450 --> 00:37:36.520
that you're protected even if
your physiological responses

00:37:36.520 --> 00:37:39.680
don't recognize that, why
should I find self crying

00:37:39.680 --> 00:37:40.920
on the train rides back home?

00:37:40.920 --> 00:37:42.580
Why does someone's story linger with me?

00:37:42.580 --> 00:37:45.670
Take up space in my
brain night after night?

00:37:45.670 --> 00:37:48.543
Nothing had after all
really happened to me.

00:37:50.520 --> 00:37:51.970
Here's where secondhand trauma

00:37:51.970 --> 00:37:55.250
does differ from first
though, I give it time,

00:37:55.250 --> 00:37:57.640
just a little, a matter of weeks or months

00:37:57.640 --> 00:38:00.570
and the hauling range
stops, the winds die down.

00:38:00.570 --> 00:38:02.210
It gets quiet enough for me to work,

00:38:02.210 --> 00:38:04.559
to put words to the things
that I saw and felt,

00:38:04.559 --> 00:38:08.710
to find the way to bring other
people into what I'm feeling.

00:38:08.710 --> 00:38:11.090
When I translate, I try
to make myself invisible,

00:38:11.090 --> 00:38:12.480
in a sense I don't exist.

00:38:12.480 --> 00:38:14.150
When I take up the weight
of someone else's story

00:38:14.150 --> 00:38:16.330
by translating it, the
thing I carry is a ghost

00:38:16.330 --> 00:38:18.300
and the teller is no
lighter for the mere fact

00:38:18.300 --> 00:38:20.644
of me carrying the story alongside them.

00:38:20.644 --> 00:38:22.460
It's in feeling the weight of the story

00:38:22.460 --> 00:38:25.506
that I can transmit it to
you also, which is why I try

00:38:25.506 --> 00:38:28.490
and why I try to erase
myself in the process

00:38:28.490 --> 00:38:29.920
so that you might read someone's words

00:38:29.920 --> 00:38:32.140
that I have translated and
be reminded of your mother

00:38:32.140 --> 00:38:35.080
or your aunt or your sister,
your neighbors and friends

00:38:35.080 --> 00:38:37.360
forgetting the step in the middle.

00:38:37.360 --> 00:38:40.040
I aim to become invisible
because then this asylum seeker

00:38:40.040 --> 00:38:41.268
is speaking directly to you

00:38:41.268 --> 00:38:44.282
and you have no choice but to listen.

00:38:44.282 --> 00:38:46.510
When I'm writing though,
working on this book

00:38:46.510 --> 00:38:49.370
or on a press release or a
client profile for my day job

00:38:49.370 --> 00:38:52.560
for this speech, this is
when I have a different goal.

00:38:52.560 --> 00:38:55.410
You for better or worse,
recognize me is one of your own.

00:38:55.410 --> 00:38:57.590
We share experiences, a
language, a way of reading

00:38:57.590 --> 00:38:58.620
or thinking about the world,

00:38:58.620 --> 00:39:00.470
that means that I've
been invited here to work

00:39:00.470 --> 00:39:03.588
and give a talk at Yale, but
I've also however, briefly

00:39:03.588 --> 00:39:07.418
and not experientially had
my foot in another world,

00:39:07.418 --> 00:39:10.223
which is the world that I'm
here to translate for you.

00:39:11.320 --> 00:39:12.590
This trauma translation,

00:39:12.590 --> 00:39:14.840
my weaker ghost version
of the original thing

00:39:14.840 --> 00:39:18.660
that witnessed, I'm hoping it
works like a real translation.

00:39:18.660 --> 00:39:20.420
In the same way that translating a text

00:39:20.420 --> 00:39:21.720
brings it to a new audience,

00:39:21.720 --> 00:39:23.340
I want my work to bring a reality

00:39:23.340 --> 00:39:26.530
that was previously invisible
and illegible to light.

00:39:26.530 --> 00:39:28.870
I'm hoping it builds a bridge
to greater understanding

00:39:28.870 --> 00:39:30.580
or rather a bridge across the gaps

00:39:30.580 --> 00:39:31.950
when you realize that like me,

00:39:31.950 --> 00:39:35.074
you understand so very little of anything.

00:39:35.074 --> 00:39:38.240
I hope it's clear that
what I'm giving you is not,

00:39:38.240 --> 00:39:40.570
cannot actually be the real thing.

00:39:40.570 --> 00:39:42.500
I couldn't even hold
the real thing myself.

00:39:42.500 --> 00:39:45.220
I felt it slipped through
my fingers as I tried.

00:39:45.220 --> 00:39:47.110
It's far from Walter Benjamin's ideal

00:39:47.110 --> 00:39:48.460
of an invisible translator,

00:39:48.460 --> 00:39:49.960
but I'm doing the only thing I can.

00:39:49.960 --> 00:39:51.620
I'm making myself visible.

00:39:51.620 --> 00:39:53.920
I'm taking your hand and
walking you through it,

00:39:53.920 --> 00:39:55.970
pointing out the places
where I've left fingers out

00:39:55.970 --> 00:40:00.032
or omitted details, critiquing
my own translating as we go.

00:40:00.032 --> 00:40:01.966
I can't go back to the person I was before

00:40:01.966 --> 00:40:04.670
that church basement in
New York, before Tijuana,

00:40:04.670 --> 00:40:05.840
before the Boston courtroom

00:40:05.840 --> 00:40:07.800
or the prison along the Mississippi.

00:40:07.800 --> 00:40:09.810
I can't unbecome my parents' daughter,

00:40:09.810 --> 00:40:13.280
I can't unlearn Spanish
or unteach myself English.

00:40:13.280 --> 00:40:15.640
And so I'm left to return
to these things in places

00:40:15.640 --> 00:40:18.300
over and over to draw strength from them

00:40:18.300 --> 00:40:20.260
to see the world more
clearly, to think of them

00:40:20.260 --> 00:40:22.460
not as the facts of a
biography but as tools

00:40:22.460 --> 00:40:23.764
with which to dismantle walls,

00:40:23.764 --> 00:40:26.110
tools with which to
flinging open the doors

00:40:26.110 --> 00:40:29.583
and beckon people in, thank you.

00:40:29.583 --> 00:40:32.750
(audience applauding)

00:40:45.266 --> 00:40:46.620
- [Woman 1] I'm wondering,
you've worked on

00:40:46.620 --> 00:40:48.560
a lot of different kinds of translation,

00:40:48.560 --> 00:40:51.990
are there kinds of translation
that you haven't worked on

00:40:51.990 --> 00:40:53.900
that you would really like to explore

00:40:53.900 --> 00:40:55.570
or things on your radar

00:40:55.570 --> 00:40:57.790
that maybe you've worked on privately

00:40:57.790 --> 00:41:00.350
that you hope to maybe bring
to your professional side?

00:41:00.350 --> 00:41:03.600
I know that you have a lot
of different directions

00:41:03.600 --> 00:41:05.270
for translation that you work on.

00:41:05.270 --> 00:41:09.172
- Yeah, I have done a fair
amount of literary translation

00:41:09.172 --> 00:41:12.690
on my own, but haven't
had a ton published.

00:41:12.690 --> 00:41:15.730
I do have a book with
Fordham University Press

00:41:15.730 --> 00:41:19.300
that I got to collaborate
with Rachel Buff,

00:41:19.300 --> 00:41:20.820
who is an immigration historian

00:41:20.820 --> 00:41:21.960
at the University of Milwaukee.

00:41:21.960 --> 00:41:23.597
And the book is called
"A is for Asylum Seeker"

00:41:23.597 --> 00:41:25.670
and it's actually a bilingual edition,

00:41:25.670 --> 00:41:27.310
which is really great.

00:41:27.310 --> 00:41:29.440
But beyond that, I haven't
really gotten to do

00:41:29.440 --> 00:41:31.193
a ton of literary translation
that's been published

00:41:31.193 --> 00:41:35.210
and edited and that I've
really got to be forced

00:41:35.210 --> 00:41:37.380
to bring all the way to completion.

00:41:37.380 --> 00:41:39.330
And so that's something

00:41:39.330 --> 00:41:41.280
that I really would like to do more of.

00:41:46.530 --> 00:41:47.920
- [Man 1] Thank you so much for that.

00:41:47.920 --> 00:41:51.430
That was a really beautiful
and very stimulating talk.

00:41:51.430 --> 00:41:54.620
I realize some members of the
audience may be more versed

00:41:54.620 --> 00:41:56.270
in all of this literature than I am,

00:41:56.270 --> 00:41:58.130
but I was just curious
if you could explain

00:41:58.130 --> 00:42:01.770
a little bit more, the
connection you described between

00:42:03.329 --> 00:42:06.080
modern translation theory and literature

00:42:06.080 --> 00:42:08.710
and Christian conceptions of suffering

00:42:08.710 --> 00:42:10.630
and the connection between those two.

00:42:10.630 --> 00:42:13.160
And I found that fascinating.

00:42:13.160 --> 00:42:17.240
- Yeah, so I think a lot of
times when you are looking at

00:42:17.240 --> 00:42:20.830
really early translation theory,

00:42:20.830 --> 00:42:23.340
a lot of the really like Saint Jerome,

00:42:23.340 --> 00:42:26.110
who was the first guy to
translate the Bible into Latin

00:42:26.110 --> 00:42:30.180
in its entirety, you're
really seeing a lot of things

00:42:30.180 --> 00:42:34.610
that are struggling with
how can we take this text

00:42:34.610 --> 00:42:37.270
that we recognize as
being completely divine,

00:42:37.270 --> 00:42:39.360
completely inviolable
and then translate it?

00:42:39.360 --> 00:42:41.240
Is that not removing it from its sanctity?

00:42:41.240 --> 00:42:43.070
Is it not carrying it away from that

00:42:43.070 --> 00:42:45.350
in a lot of the same
ways that we talk about

00:42:45.350 --> 00:42:48.556
translations as being
less correct versions

00:42:48.556 --> 00:42:53.289
of the original text or
adulterated versions.

00:42:53.289 --> 00:42:55.830
There's a quote, I think from Servantis,

00:42:55.830 --> 00:42:57.920
who describes a translation

00:42:57.920 --> 00:43:00.920
is a back of an embroidery actually,

00:43:00.920 --> 00:43:03.663
and showing all the
seams and the ugly sides.

00:43:05.053 --> 00:43:10.053
And so when you are
referring to the original

00:43:10.300 --> 00:43:12.850
in this inviolable light,
because it's divine,

00:43:12.850 --> 00:43:14.970
because it's perfect, even
when you're talking about

00:43:14.970 --> 00:43:18.450
something other than a scriptural text,

00:43:18.450 --> 00:43:23.450
you're still giving it this
authority and this divinity

00:43:25.170 --> 00:43:27.980
that gets really complicated
and gets really muddy

00:43:27.980 --> 00:43:32.730
when what you're talking
about is someone's account

00:43:32.730 --> 00:43:35.600
of suffering that was
extracted from them willingly

00:43:35.600 --> 00:43:37.880
in order so that they
might fill out this form.

00:43:37.880 --> 00:43:42.880
And I think that Christian
scripture especially is really,

00:43:43.720 --> 00:43:45.500
there's a lot of emphasis on suffering,

00:43:45.500 --> 00:43:47.370
in particular being pure and being divine

00:43:47.370 --> 00:43:49.240
and being one of the ways
that we attain holiness,

00:43:49.240 --> 00:43:50.930
like just through the Christ story alone

00:43:50.930 --> 00:43:54.420
is a pretty clear example of that.

00:43:54.420 --> 00:43:56.300
And so when you kind
of lay things together,

00:43:56.300 --> 00:43:58.811
things get really, really muddy

00:43:58.811 --> 00:44:01.360
if you're looking at
this suffering as holy

00:44:01.360 --> 00:44:04.703
when we should really be
regarding it as anything but.

00:44:10.630 --> 00:44:12.360
- [Woman 2] Is this
on? (Woman 2 chuckling)

00:44:12.360 --> 00:44:14.810
I wondered whether the words
that you just shared with us,

00:44:14.810 --> 00:44:18.190
your speech today is in
the book that's coming out.

00:44:18.190 --> 00:44:19.023
- Yeah.

00:44:19.023 --> 00:44:19.856
- [Woman 2] Because I wanna share it

00:44:19.856 --> 00:44:21.030
with some other interpreters that I know.

00:44:21.030 --> 00:44:22.125
- Oh, wonderful.

00:44:22.125 --> 00:44:22.958
(Alejandra chuckling)

00:44:22.958 --> 00:44:23.791
- [Woman 2] And I don't
know if it's separate

00:44:23.791 --> 00:44:24.730
or in the book.

00:44:24.730 --> 00:44:28.920
- It's in the book, but I
was copying and pasting a lot

00:44:28.920 --> 00:44:30.320
from different sections of the book.

00:44:30.320 --> 00:44:32.140
There's some things in here
that don't appear in the book.

00:44:32.140 --> 00:44:35.540
Like the book is, it's a Venn diagram

00:44:35.540 --> 00:44:37.010
that's very closely overlapped

00:44:37.010 --> 00:44:38.550
but there's still some
stuff outside of it,

00:44:38.550 --> 00:44:42.310
but I think this is going to
be on YouTube later, so yeah.

00:44:46.120 --> 00:44:47.610
- [Man 2] Hi, thank you so much for coming

00:44:47.610 --> 00:44:49.550
and speaking with us.

00:44:49.550 --> 00:44:51.797
You cited early Walter Benjamin's,

00:44:51.797 --> 00:44:54.500
"The Task of the Translator"
and one of his core arguments

00:44:54.500 --> 00:44:57.470
in that text is that it's not just merely

00:44:57.470 --> 00:45:01.610
that the translator's role
is to translate a work

00:45:01.610 --> 00:45:03.750
from a language A to language B,

00:45:03.750 --> 00:45:06.620
but that that act of translation in fact

00:45:06.620 --> 00:45:10.603
changes both those languages
and the work itself.

00:45:11.520 --> 00:45:13.400
And I actually am a
student at the law school

00:45:13.400 --> 00:45:16.130
and I recently had the opportunity
to do intake interviews

00:45:16.130 --> 00:45:17.550
for asylum seekers.

00:45:17.550 --> 00:45:22.360
And so I'm curious having gone through

00:45:22.360 --> 00:45:25.690
that translation process
myself in a smaller form,

00:45:25.690 --> 00:45:28.670
what is your process of
translation as well as navigating

00:45:28.670 --> 00:45:30.670
your ethical obligations of the translator

00:45:30.670 --> 00:45:35.670
when your trying to take
what these clients of yours

00:45:36.630 --> 00:45:40.400
are trying to share with you
and as you translate that

00:45:40.400 --> 00:45:44.410
and change it in a separate language

00:45:44.410 --> 00:45:49.410
and as well into the box that
basically the US government

00:45:49.610 --> 00:45:52.450
wants you to be able to
fit their narrative in

00:45:52.450 --> 00:45:54.730
and also trying to create
a compelling narrative

00:45:54.730 --> 00:45:56.510
for the government.

00:45:56.510 --> 00:45:59.467
- Yeah, so this was something
that I really struggled with

00:45:59.467 --> 00:46:03.540
and I still really struggle
with as I'm in my day job,

00:46:03.540 --> 00:46:05.260
I am the Communications Coordinator

00:46:05.260 --> 00:46:06.810
for the National Immigrant Justice Center.

00:46:06.810 --> 00:46:08.540
I end up interviewing a lot of people.

00:46:08.540 --> 00:46:11.570
And while they get to tell
somewhat expanded stories

00:46:11.570 --> 00:46:14.510
of and about themselves for
things like the media and stuff,

00:46:14.510 --> 00:46:17.860
there's still always
sensitivities around the case

00:46:17.860 --> 00:46:18.870
that we want to be like,

00:46:18.870 --> 00:46:20.340
okay, I know this feels important for you

00:46:20.340 --> 00:46:22.690
but you cannot share this in a wider venue

00:46:22.690 --> 00:46:24.180
in case it impacts your case

00:46:24.180 --> 00:46:27.410
or people are going to read
this in a different way

00:46:27.410 --> 00:46:29.760
than what you are wanting
to share about yourself.

00:46:29.760 --> 00:46:34.760
And then it's also really
hard to actually get someone's

00:46:37.130 --> 00:46:42.030
full informed consent to do the
translation and storytelling

00:46:42.030 --> 00:46:44.440
that you're doing, because you're like,

00:46:44.440 --> 00:46:45.610
this is what I need from you,

00:46:45.610 --> 00:46:48.320
this is what the US
government needs from you.

00:46:48.320 --> 00:46:51.650
And I know that what feels
important to you about you story

00:46:51.650 --> 00:46:52.839
is maybe not the same things

00:46:52.839 --> 00:46:56.020
as what this other entity
feels is important.

00:46:56.020 --> 00:47:01.020
And so the best answer, the
best workaround I've found

00:47:01.290 --> 00:47:03.100
is to just read back,

00:47:03.100 --> 00:47:07.400
retranslate whatever I've
come up with back into Spanish

00:47:07.400 --> 00:47:10.150
for them and be like, okay,
here's what I have for you,

00:47:10.150 --> 00:47:12.219
I know it's not going
to say the whole thing,

00:47:12.219 --> 00:47:15.410
I know it's not how you
would tell your story

00:47:15.410 --> 00:47:16.870
because I've heard you tell your story

00:47:16.870 --> 00:47:18.470
and this is not how you tell it,

00:47:18.470 --> 00:47:21.920
but I'm going to give
you back what I've done.

00:47:21.920 --> 00:47:24.260
And tell me if there's something
that you want to change,

00:47:24.260 --> 00:47:26.680
keeping in mind that we have
all of these bullet points

00:47:26.680 --> 00:47:29.690
that we need to hit in order
to make this the strongest case

00:47:29.690 --> 00:47:31.270
that we can for you.

00:47:31.270 --> 00:47:34.270
And so it's just about
talking back and forth

00:47:34.270 --> 00:47:35.485
as much as possible and being like,

00:47:35.485 --> 00:47:38.440
here's what I'm hearing from you,

00:47:38.440 --> 00:47:40.460
here's what I need to
share with the government,

00:47:40.460 --> 00:47:41.360
here's what the government

00:47:41.360 --> 00:47:42.960
is going to know about you eventually,

00:47:42.960 --> 00:47:44.620
or what this journalist is going to know

00:47:44.620 --> 00:47:46.290
or what we can share with this journalist

00:47:46.290 --> 00:47:49.230
and what would be better if
we don't talk to about them

00:47:49.230 --> 00:47:50.693
or whatever's going on.

00:47:55.270 --> 00:47:57.060
- [Woman 3] Yeah, I
wanna continue on that.

00:47:57.060 --> 00:47:58.480
Because as you were speaking,

00:47:58.480 --> 00:48:03.070
there was this incredible
suspense you created in your talk.

00:48:03.070 --> 00:48:06.240
And I was imagining the form in my mind.

00:48:06.240 --> 00:48:09.500
I mean, I could see the
paper and I was imagining

00:48:09.500 --> 00:48:14.500
how in the world, how
Miriam's story came out.

00:48:14.540 --> 00:48:19.240
And I started thinking about
narrative and about restraint

00:48:19.240 --> 00:48:22.810
and the kind of restraint you have to use.

00:48:22.810 --> 00:48:24.260
I mean, you've answered it a little

00:48:24.260 --> 00:48:29.260
but I guess what I wonder
is say in your book,

00:48:29.370 --> 00:48:33.120
are you gonna include some of those forms?

00:48:33.120 --> 00:48:34.180
- Oh, that's a good question.

00:48:34.180 --> 00:48:37.210
I don't currently have
any in there right now.

00:48:37.210 --> 00:48:38.790
I do have some of the conversations

00:48:38.790 --> 00:48:39.990
that I was describing of like,

00:48:39.990 --> 00:48:41.700
okay, I'm gonna read you your story back

00:48:41.700 --> 00:48:46.210
or like, let's have this
conversation about what's going on.

00:48:46.210 --> 00:48:50.910
And there are also all different
kinds of ways that people

00:48:50.910 --> 00:48:53.310
respond to having to tell this story.

00:48:53.310 --> 00:48:54.290
I've sat there with people

00:48:54.290 --> 00:48:56.240
who are just telling joke
after joke after joke

00:48:56.240 --> 00:48:58.990
of horrifying things that
are happening to them.

00:48:58.990 --> 00:49:03.990
I've heard of people who just
stare down at their hands,

00:49:04.310 --> 00:49:06.910
do a monotone, I'm going
to get this all out,

00:49:06.910 --> 00:49:08.480
it's going to come out once and that's it,

00:49:08.480 --> 00:49:10.903
no questions we're done here.

00:49:12.240 --> 00:49:14.420
I've had people who cry
through the whole thing.

00:49:14.420 --> 00:49:16.920
There's just such a variety of ways

00:49:16.920 --> 00:49:19.190
that people encounter this questioning

00:49:20.449 --> 00:49:23.750
that again you're like, I
want to be able to take this

00:49:23.750 --> 00:49:28.750
and like show the government
this person is so altered

00:49:29.230 --> 00:49:30.510
by having passed through this.

00:49:30.510 --> 00:49:32.410
And their storytelling is so altered

00:49:32.410 --> 00:49:34.080
by having gone through this experience

00:49:34.080 --> 00:49:36.220
and there's just no
way to get that across.

00:49:36.220 --> 00:49:38.760
And some people end up
eventually getting to go to court

00:49:38.760 --> 00:49:40.850
and having their hearing
and being able to testify

00:49:40.850 --> 00:49:43.360
on their own behalf
but that's pretty rare.

00:49:43.360 --> 00:49:46.880
- [Woman 3] Yeah, it's
heartbreaking that there's no room

00:49:46.880 --> 00:49:51.070
for storytelling and yet
you need to move somebody,

00:49:51.070 --> 00:49:54.350
somebody on the other end
is reading and being moved

00:49:54.350 --> 00:49:55.960
or reading between the lines.

00:49:55.960 --> 00:49:56.793
Yeah, thank you.

00:50:09.580 --> 00:50:10.413
- [Woman 4] Thank you so much

00:50:10.413 --> 00:50:11.810
for sharing this story with us.

00:50:11.810 --> 00:50:14.190
I am wondering where your relationship

00:50:14.190 --> 00:50:15.900
ends with each of these clients.

00:50:15.900 --> 00:50:17.930
Does it stop after they fill out this form

00:50:17.930 --> 00:50:19.910
or do you continue to help
them through their process

00:50:19.910 --> 00:50:21.070
and get updates on

00:50:21.070 --> 00:50:23.433
whether their application
was approved or not?

00:50:24.380 --> 00:50:28.240
- That really depends on what
I'm working on them with.

00:50:28.240 --> 00:50:31.190
So when I was working
at the Pro Se Clinic,

00:50:31.190 --> 00:50:33.642
it was very much like, you
have your paperwork go file it.

00:50:33.642 --> 00:50:36.390
We were not attorneys, we
were not really qualified

00:50:36.390 --> 00:50:38.870
to help them through any of the
other stages of the process.

00:50:38.870 --> 00:50:42.350
And so every once in a while
someone would come back

00:50:42.350 --> 00:50:45.850
and be like, this happened,
or this is going on.

00:50:45.850 --> 00:50:48.420
I do have a lot more
contact with the folks

00:50:48.420 --> 00:50:49.300
that I work with now

00:50:49.300 --> 00:50:50.630
at the National Immigrant Justice Center,

00:50:50.630 --> 00:50:53.173
because I do work with
a lot of attorneys who,

00:50:54.007 --> 00:50:56.028
there have been times, I actually got to,

00:50:56.028 --> 00:50:57.860
there's an AP story about this

00:50:57.860 --> 00:50:59.160
that I did the translation for

00:50:59.160 --> 00:51:02.580
but we had a client named
Alex who had been in detention

00:51:02.580 --> 00:51:04.690
for a really, really long time.

00:51:04.690 --> 00:51:07.240
He was a gay man and he had been,

00:51:07.240 --> 00:51:09.570
the guards there were just
kind of pushing him around

00:51:09.570 --> 00:51:11.370
and being really mean to him.

00:51:11.370 --> 00:51:16.360
And I said that they were gonna
let him go and they didn't.

00:51:16.360 --> 00:51:20.640
And there was all this push
pull going on with that.

00:51:20.640 --> 00:51:23.077
And then there got to a
point where he was like,

00:51:23.077 --> 00:51:25.080
"I'm done, I wanna talk to a reporter,

00:51:25.080 --> 00:51:26.040
let's get this set up."

00:51:26.040 --> 00:51:27.920
And so I was able to work on interpreting

00:51:27.920 --> 00:51:28.753
through that with him.

00:51:28.753 --> 00:51:31.340
And then a couple months ago actually,

00:51:31.340 --> 00:51:35.100
he got out and got reunited
with his boyfriend,

00:51:35.100 --> 00:51:36.780
he's living very happily.

00:51:36.780 --> 00:51:38.690
And so it's very occasionally

00:51:38.690 --> 00:51:41.590
that I get to hear the
endpoint of someone's story.

00:51:41.590 --> 00:51:44.400
But especially now that I'm working

00:51:44.400 --> 00:51:46.510
with a legal aid organization,

00:51:46.510 --> 00:51:48.887
very often they are
good ending to stories.

00:51:48.887 --> 00:51:50.437
And that's really, really nice.

00:51:58.071 --> 00:52:00.772
I think we're set, oh,
Hailey's coming back.

00:52:00.772 --> 00:52:01.880
(Alejandra laughing)

00:52:01.880 --> 00:52:04.453
- [Woman 5] Time for one
more, oh Hailey has one.

00:52:08.634 --> 00:52:10.526
- Hailey has one.

00:52:10.526 --> 00:52:14.210
(Alejandra chuckling)

00:52:14.210 --> 00:52:16.480
- [Hailey] So I know that the
book that you're working on

00:52:16.480 --> 00:52:20.340
right now is coming out
and I'm curious in terms of

00:52:20.340 --> 00:52:25.340
how much of that includes your
experience with immigration

00:52:26.470 --> 00:52:29.460
versus translation theory versus,

00:52:29.460 --> 00:52:32.270
I know you touched on some
of the Christianity roots

00:52:32.270 --> 00:52:37.270
of translation and how do you balance

00:52:37.960 --> 00:52:40.513
how much you include of those pieces?

00:52:41.400 --> 00:52:43.662
- By having a very good editor.

00:52:43.662 --> 00:52:45.390
(audience laughing)

00:52:45.390 --> 00:52:47.210
I gotta say that a lot of my early drafts

00:52:47.210 --> 00:52:50.350
are pretty heavily lopsided
in one direction or another

00:52:50.350 --> 00:52:51.470
and they're like, "Okay, hold on,

00:52:51.470 --> 00:52:53.140
you have to bring this other thread back

00:52:53.140 --> 00:52:56.641
or you have to talk about
this a little more."

00:52:56.641 --> 00:53:01.640
I also know that I know
a lot about this stuff

00:53:01.640 --> 00:53:03.700
but sometimes forget
that other people don't.

00:53:03.700 --> 00:53:05.520
And so I am lucky enough

00:53:05.520 --> 00:53:08.607
to work with a really
fantastic agent who is like,

00:53:08.607 --> 00:53:11.200
"I don't know what this
means, you need to explain

00:53:11.200 --> 00:53:13.620
what a credible fear
interview is a little better

00:53:13.620 --> 00:53:14.480
or something like that."

00:53:14.480 --> 00:53:15.880
And so I've been really, really lucky

00:53:15.880 --> 00:53:18.300
to work with other people who
have been fantastic readers

00:53:18.300 --> 00:53:20.250
and asking me good questions

00:53:20.250 --> 00:53:23.010
to make sure that
everything's well balanced

00:53:23.010 --> 00:53:25.160
because that's not
always my first impulse.

00:53:27.030 --> 00:53:29.570
- [Woman 6] Well, we wish
you just huge success

00:53:29.570 --> 00:53:31.960
with this important
book and thank you again

00:53:31.960 --> 00:53:35.800
for sharing your story and
the story of your clients.

00:53:35.800 --> 00:53:37.132
- Thank you so much.

00:53:37.132 --> 00:53:40.299
(audience applauding)

